                   "AF": "FA", "CA": "AC", "CB": "BC", "DC": "CD",
                   "GF": "FG", "FE": "EF", "ACB": "ABC", "ADC": "ACD" },
        slides: [
            { text: "“In any parallelogram the parallelograms about the diameter are similar both to the whole and to one another.”",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","AC","EG","HK"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "VI.24" }] },
            { text: "Let {ABCD|ABCDt} be a parallelogram, and {AC} its diameter,",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","AC"] },
            { text: "and let {EG} and {HK} be parallelograms about {AC}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["EG","HK","AC"] },
            { text: "I say that each of the parallelograms {EG} and {HK} is similar both to the whole {ABCD|ABCDt} and to the other.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["EG","HK","ABCDt"] },
            { text: "For, since {EF} is parallel to a side {BC} of the triangle {ABC},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["EF","BC","ABC"] },
            { text: "proportionally, {BE} is to {EA} as {CF} is to {FA}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["BE","EA","CF","FA"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "BE : EA = CF : FA", ref: "VI.2" }] },
            { text: "Again, since {FG} is parallel to a side {CD} of the triangle {ACD},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["FG","CD","ACD"] },
            { text: "proportionally, {CF} is to {FA} as {DG} is to {GA}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["CF","FA","DG","GA"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "CF : FA = DG : GA", ref: "VI.2" }] },
            { text: "But it was proved that {CF} is to {FA} as {BE} is to {EA},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["CF","FA","BE","EA"] },
            { text: "therefore {BE} is to {EA} as {DG} is to {GA}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["BE","EA","DG","GA"] },
            { text: "Therefore, taken jointly, {BA} is to {AE} as {DA} is to {AG},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["BA","EA","DA","GA"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "jointly, BA : AE = DA : AG", ref: "V.18" }] },
            { text: "and, alternately, {BA} is to {AD} as {EA} is to {AG}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["BA","DA","EA","GA"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "alternately, BA : AD = EA : AG", ref: "V.16" }] },
            { text: "Therefore in the parallelograms {ABCD|ABCDt} and {EG}, the sides about the common angle {BAD|angBAD} are proportional.",
              visible: BAD,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","EG","angBAD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angBAD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "And, since {GF} is parallel to {DC}, the angle {AFG|angAFG} equals the angle {ACD|angACD},",
              visible: BAD.concat(["angAFG","angACD"]),
              highlighted: ["FG","CD","angAFG","angACD"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angAFG", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angACD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "angle AFG = angle ACD", ref: "I.29" }] },
            { text: "and the angle {DAC|angDAC} is common to the two triangles {ADC} and {AGF},",
              visible: EQ1,
              highlighted: ["angDAC","ACD","AGF"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angDAC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "therefore the triangle {ADC} is equiangular with the triangle {AGF}.",
              visible: EQ1,
              highlighted: ["ACD","AGF"] },
            { text: "For the same reason the triangle {ACB} is also equiangular with the triangle {AFE},",
              visible: EQ2,
              highlighted: ["ABC","AFE","angCAB","angAFE","angACB"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angCAB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angAFE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angACB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and the whole parallelogram {ABCD|ABCDt} is equiangular with the parallelogram {EG}.",
              visible: EQ2,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","EG"] },
            { text: "Therefore, proportionally, {AD} is to {DC} as {AG} is to {GF},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["DA","CD","GA","FG"] },
            { text: "{DC} is to {CA} as {GF} is to {FA},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["CD","AC","FG","FA"] },
            { text: "{AC} is to {CB} as {AF} is to {FE},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["AC","BC","FA","EF"] },
            { text: "and {CB} is to {BA} as {FE} is to {EA}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["BC","BA","EF","EA"] },
            { text: "And, since it was proved that {DC} is to {CA} as {GF} is to {FA},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["CD","AC","FG","FA"] },
            { text: "and {AC} is to {CB} as {AF} is to {FE},",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["AC","BC","FA","EF"] },
            { text: "therefore, ex aequali, {DC} is to {CB} as {GF} is to {FE}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["CD","BC","FG","EF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "ex aequali, DC : CB = GF : FE", ref: "V.22" }] },
            { text: "Therefore in the parallelograms {ABCD|ABCDt} and {EG} the sides about the equal angles are proportional.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","EG"] },
            { text: "Therefore the parallelogram {ABCD|ABCDt} is similar to the parallelogram {EG}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","EG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "ABCD similar to EG", ref: "VI.Def.1" }] },
            { text: "For the same reason the parallelogram {ABCD|ABCDt} is also similar to the parallelogram {KH|HK}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","HK"] },
            { text: "Therefore each of the parallelograms {EG} and {HK} is similar to {ABCD|ABCDt}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["EG","HK","ABCDt"] },
            { text: "But figures similar to the same rectilinear figure are also similar to one another,",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["EG","HK","ABCDt"] },
            { text: "therefore the parallelogram {EG} is also similar to the parallelogram {HK}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["EG","HK"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "EG similar to HK", ref: "VI.21" }] },
            { text: "Therefore, in any parallelogram the parallelograms about the diameter are similar both to the whole and to one another.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCDt","AC","EG","HK"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "VI.24") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.29":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI29/",
        "V.16":     "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV16/",
        "V.18":     "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV18/",
        "V.22":     "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV22/",
        "VI.2":     "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI2/",
        "VI.21":    "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI21/",
        "VI.Def.1": "/elements/books/bookVI/definitions/defVI1/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

For, since {EF} is parallel to a side {BC} of the triangle {ABC}, proportionally, {BE} is to {EA} as {CF} is to {FA}. [!just VI.2]

Again, since {FG} is parallel to a side {CD} of the triangle {ACD}, proportionally, {CF} is to {FA} as {DG} is to {GA}. [!just VI.2]

But it was proved that {CF} is to {FA} as {BE} is to {EA}, therefore {BE} is to {EA} as {DG} is to {GA}.  Therefore, taken jointly, {BA} is to {AE} as {DA} is to {AG}, and, alternately, {BA} is to {AD} as {EA} is to {AG}. [!just V.18; V.16]

Therefore in the parallelograms {ABCD|ABCDt} and {EG}, the sides about the common angle {BAD|angBAD} are proportional.

And, since {GF} is parallel to {DC}, the angle {AFG|angAFG} equals the angle {ACD|angACD}, and the angle {DAC|angDAC} is common to the two triangles {ADC} and {AGF}, therefore the triangle {ADC} is equiangular with the triangle {AGF}. [!just I.29]

For the same reason the triangle {ACB} is also equiangular with the triangle {AFE}, and the whole parallelogram {ABCD|ABCDt} is equiangular with the parallelogram {EG}.

Therefore, proportionally, {AD} is to {DC} as {AG} is to {GF}, {DC} is to {CA} as {GF} is to {FA}, {AC} is to {CB} as {AF} is to {FE}, and  {CB} is to {BA} as {FE} is to {EA}.

And, since it was proved that {DC} is to {CA} as {GF} is to {FA}, and {AC} is to {CB} as {AF} is to {FE}, therefore, *ex aequali,* {DC} is to {CB} as {GF} is to {FE}. [!just V.22]

Therefore in the parallelograms {ABCD|ABCDt} and {EG} the sides about the equal angles are proportional.  Therefore the parallelogram {ABCD|ABCDt} is similar to the parallelogram {EG}. [!just VI.Def.1]

For the same reason the parallelogram {ABCD|ABCDt} is also similar to the parallelogram {KH|HK}.  Therefore each of the parallelograms {EG} and {HK} is similar to {ABCD|ABCDt}.

But figures similar to the same rectilinear figure are also similar to one another, therefore the parallelogram {EG} is also similar to the parallelogram {HK}. [!just VI.21]

Therefore, *in any parallelogram the parallelograms about the diameter are similar both to the whole and to one another.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide: With this proposition Euclid returns to applications of areas.  Back in proposition @I.45 rectilinear areas were applied to lines.  In the upcoming propositions @VI.28 and @VI.29, rectilinear areas will be applied to lines but the areas will fall short of or extend beyond the end of the lines. Those propositions geometric solve two kinds of quadratic equations. This proposition is preparatory to them.  It is used in the proofs of @VI.26 (its converse) and VI.29.
